  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Mild weather pattern under southwest flow into next week. \n\n-  Periods of valley fog and stratus, especially in western \n   Montana. \n\n-  Weather system to bring shower activity, minor precipitation, \n   and high elevation snow on Sunday. \n\nA weak weather system has been pushing eastward across the\nNorthern Rockies this afternoon. Shower activity has been trending\ndownward in the last few hours, with area rain gauges showing the\nmajority of measurable precipitation along the Idaho/Montana\nborder, with a trace to 0.10 inches reported. Valley stratus\ncontinues to impact many valley areas in western Montana,\nincluding Missoula and Kalispell. The stratus deck may temporarily\nimprove for a handful of locations into this evening, but \novernight conditions will once again become favorable for valley \nstratus and fog, especially in northwest Montana where warm lake \ntemperatures over Flathead Lake are helping to saturate the \nboundary layer. \n\nModel guidance is strong agreement for high pressure to rebuild\nacross the region Friday into Saturday. This pattern once again\nappears favorable for patchy valley fog or low stratus clouds,\nespecially in western Montana. This potential brings higher\nuncertainty into the high temperature forecast for Saturday, with\ntemperatures potentially being to warm in the forecast shall\nstratus clouds and fog become widespread as they have in the last\nfew days.\n\nThe next weather system will track through the Northwest, moving\nalong the Canadian border Sunday. A trough passage will breakdown\nthe ridge, with shower activity bringing minor precipitation and\nhigh elevation (above 5,500 feet) snow along the Idaho/Montana \nborder and in Northwest Montana. Wind will also increase, with \nvalley gusts up to 25 mph as higher elevations experience gusts of\n30-40 mph. \n\nEnsemble cluster analysis points towards high pressure rebuilding\nonce again to start next week. This pattern once again points\ntowards areas of valley fog and stratus.",
